  "description": "meme-id: jokes-dont-kill-people-meme; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme uses a close-up image of a mosquito biting human skin, juxtaposed with the text 'jokes don't kill people' at the top and 'they kill people' at the bottom. The visual metaphor implies that while jokes are harmless, mosquitoes (which transmit deadly diseases) are the real killers — suggesting that jokes are not dangerous, but the act of biting (or the disease it spreads) is. The intent is to humorously downplay the perceived danger of jokes by contrasting them with a biological threat. While not overtly toxic or hateful, the meme's 'harmful' intent stems from its potential to trivialize serious issues (like disease transmission) or to be misused to justify harmful behavior (e.g., dismissing the seriousness of jokes in sensitive contexts). The meme does not contain any abusive, cyberbullying, misogynistic, or propagandistic elements, nor does it target any specific protected group. Its humor is based on a literal visual analogy, not on stereotypes or harmful tropes.",
